  "result": "{\"seen_before\": false, \"matches\": [], \"answer\": \"I found no past ticket that reports this exact problem. That problem is the generated 'PowerShell Preview (msix)' profile storing the app execution alias path. The handed-off process runs from the versioned path under C:\\\\Program Files\\\\WindowsApps, so the two never match.\\n\\nTwo older tickets have the same root cause, a profile command line that differs from the path actually launched. They are terminal#15471 (the Visual Studio Debug Console) and terminal#12582 (the WSL package path from the Start Menu). Both are about different programs, and neither covers MSIX alias resolution. Neither has a fix you can apply, and #15471 asked the owning team to change the profile.\\n\\nUntil this is fixed, your hidden-profile workaround is the best option. You will need to update its package path after each PowerShell Preview update. Please file this as a new issue with your proposed solution, which is to resolve AppExecLink targets or match on package family name. Link #15471 and #12582 in it.\\n\\nTicket #20333 involves the same generated profile, but it is a different problem. There the profile doesn't appear in the profile list at all.\"}",
